Two ci.yml jobs red on the same push (run 30638444619, head d293b951), both
green on a developer laptop, both failing for the same underlying reason.

1. Job "signature-parity", step "Electron + Tauri runner parity":

  FAIL  ./rn/runner.mjs: bundle does not import: Cannot find package
  'webdriverio' imported from /home/runner/work/reproit/reproit/runners/rn/runner.mjs
  ##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.

  runners/signature_test.mjs imports the host-pure signatureOf/descriptorOf out
  of each runner bundle. The React Native bundle carried a TOP-LEVEL
  `import{remote as Yt}from"webdriverio"`, so loading those pure functions
  required the Appium driver. The signature-parity job runs no `npm ci` at all,
  by design. Reproduced locally by checking out the parent into a worktree,
  where runners/rn/node_modules does not exist, giving the identical message.

2. Job "sdk-backend-core", step "python (reproit-backend-py)":

  >       assert batch["deployment"] == {"version": "1.2.3"}
  E       AssertionError: assert {'version': '...cb7740a5f857'} == {'version': '1.2.3'}
  E         Left contains 1 more item:
  E         {'commit': 'f857cb7740a5f857cb7740a5f857cb7740a5f857'}

  Capture.resolve_commit deliberately falls back to REPROIT_COMMIT and then
  GITHUB_SHA. GITHUB_SHA is always set on a GitHub runner and never set locally,
  so the batch grew a `commit` key only in CI. Reproduced locally with:

    GITHUB_SHA=f857cb7740a5f857cb7740a5f857cb7740a5f857 \
      uv run --group test -m pytest tests/test_capture.py -q
    1 failed, 5 passed

After the fix, same commands:

  node runners/signature_test.mjs, with runners/rn/node_modules moved aside:
    PASS  electron.mjs: 25/25 vectors
    PASS  tauri.mjs: 25/25 vectors
    PASS  web/runner.mjs: 25/25 vectors
    PASS  rn/runner.mjs: 25/25 vectors
    All 25 vectors pass for all 4 runners.   (rc=0)

  The dynamic import survives bundling rather than being deleted:
    let{remote:u}=await import("webdriverio"),a=await u({hostname:t.hostname,...
  and resolves at runtime: `remote is function`.

  The rn runner's own suite is unaffected: 63 pass, 0 fail.

  Python suite, with and without GITHUB_SHA set: 42 passed in both.

The regression test at the parent with only itself overlaid, which is how the
policy gate evaluates it:

  python3 validation/self-dogfood/test_gate_environment_independence.py
  test_the_python_capture_suite_clears_ambient_code_identity ... FAIL
  test_the_rn_bundle_loads_without_the_appium_driver ... FAIL
  test_the_rn_bundle_still_reaches_the_driver_when_it_needs_one ... FAIL
  Ran 3 tests
  FAILED (failures=3)
  affected rc=1

3. Workflow "native-backend-gates", job "windows-uia": never ran at all.

  The first two cases above were RED. This one produced no signal of any
  kind, which is why it survived far longer. windows-uia is pinned to
  [self-hosted, macOS, ARM64, reproit-windows-bridge] and its condition was
  `github.event_name != 'workflow_dispatch' || inputs.run_windows_uia`, so
  it ran on every push and was SKIPPED on the one event that can opt in.
  Its own declared dispatch default is false, so the condition inverted the
  author's stated intent.

  Registered self-hosted runners for this repository:
    total_count = 0

  So the job could never be scheduled, and a GitHub run stays queued until
  every job concludes. Observed on 2026-07-31, most recent 12 runs:

    11f819e  queued    2026-07-31T15:08:16Z
    31bc358  queued    2026-07-31T15:04:52Z
    d293b95  queued    2026-07-31T14:24:15Z
    94c13cb  queued    2026-07-31T14:12:16Z
    1d57250  queued    2026-07-31T14:11:04Z
    6695474  queued    2026-07-31T13:43:36Z
    96c7007  queued    2026-07-31T12:18:38Z
    d7e996a  queued    2026-07-31T12:04:46Z
    5648d90  queued    2026-07-31T11:49:49Z
    655a95e  queued    2026-07-31T11:17:47Z
    efe1254  queued    2026-07-31T10:55:35Z
    6229e44  queued    2026-07-31T10:18:05Z

  Twelve consecutive runs, hours apart, none of which ever reported red or
  green. This is the same failure class as the vacuous acceptance harnesses
  check-harness-integrity.py exists for: a gate that cannot report is
  indistinguishable from one that is merely slow, so it reads as fine.
  Fixed by matching macos-ax, which was already correctly opt-in.

4. Job "signature-parity", step "Linux native SDK parity": ModuleNotFoundError.

  ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'reproit_linux'

  sdk/reproit-linux/tests/test_behavior_vectors.py imported its own
  package by name. CI runs it as a bare path from the repository root:
    283:          python3 sdk/reproit-linux/tests/test_parity.py
    284:          python3 sdk/reproit-linux/tests/test_capture.py
    285:          python3 sdk/reproit-linux/tests/test_context.py
    286:          python3 sdk/reproit-linux/tests/test_behavior_vectors.py

  Three sibling suites were fine for three different reasons, so there was
  no single house style to copy:
    sdk/reproit-backend-py   working-directory: sdk/reproit-backend-py
    sdk/reproit-tui-py       PYTHONPATH=sdk/reproit-tui-py
    sdk/reproit-linux/...    in-file sys.path.insert (test_capture.py)
  and test_parity.py needs none of them because it loads modules by
  explicit file path through importlib.

  The fifth structural case pins the rule. Its FIRST version was vacuous:
  it searched for the bare string "sys.path", which also appears in the
  comment explaining the insert, so it passed with the defect put back.
  The negative control caught that. It now matches the statement:

    negative control, sys.path.insert removed -> FAILED (failures=1)
    restored                                  -> OK (5 cases)

5. Job "sdk-backend-ports" on b962186: the Java SDK, first push after gating.

  org.opentest4j.AssertionFailedError:
    expected: <{version=1.2.3}>
    but was:  <{version=1.2.3, commit=b962186ec5105623c171ba40881801672c1087a3}>
  at dev.reproit.backend.CaptureTest.serverErrorBatchUsesUniversalCausalContract

The SAME defect as case 2, in a different language, found six commits later.
Capture.resolveCommit falls back to REPROIT_COMMIT then GITHUB_SHA, which is
correct behaviour; a GitHub runner always sets GITHUB_SHA and a laptop never
does, so the batch grew a commit key only in CI. It surfaced now and not
earlier for one reason: this SDK had never been release-gating. Abolishing
the support tiers is what exposed it.

Java cannot unset its own environment in-process, so the fix mirrors the
Python SDK's seam rather than inventing a new one: resolveCommit gains an
overload taking the environment explicitly, backed by a replaceable
Capture.environment field, and the suite swaps in an empty map per test.
The fallback it used to exercise by accident is now pinned on purpose by
aCiRunnerSuppliesTheCommitTheConfigOmits.

Measured both ways, real exit codes:
  GITHUB_SHA=b962186... mvn -B test  -> CaptureTest 7/7, BUILD SUCCESS
  env -u GITHUB_SHA     mvn -B test  -> CaptureTest 7/7, BUILD SUCCESS

Control on the seventh structural case:
  Capture.environment replacement removed -> guard exit 1
  restored                                -> guard exit 0

Checked the sibling SDKs for the same shape while here: only the Rust SDK
asserts a deployment, and it asserts the version FIELD rather than the whole
map, so an extra commit key cannot break it. No other SDK was affected.
